  "design": {
    "medintro": {
      "n3": ".",
      "title": "Medical — the clinic component library",
      "lead": "Ninety-six components for clinic software — from a patient's name to the four-pane workstation shell — built on one token set and five rules. Every story here is live: poke the controls, resize the viewport, switch the locale. If a component can't survive you doing that, it's a bug.",
      "orgTitle": "How the sidebar is organized",
      "o1": " — the pure pieces: formatters, flags, dots, pills, the form kit, the search inputs, the table primitive. If you're building a clinical surface, these are your vocabulary words.",
      "o2": " — the frames: the app shell, both navigation rails, panels, the paper surface, and the three screen blueprints that prove the parts compose into real screens.",
      "o3": " — the organisms: queues, schedules, results, timelines, orders, documents, messages, the specialist tools. Real data, real interactions, built from the vocabulary above.",
      "fiveTitle": "Open these five first",
      "f1": " — the tone of the whole library: loud where it must be, honest about what it doesn't know.",
      "f2": " — the dense table eleven other components are built on. Learn its props once, read every table forever.",
      "f3": " — two channels (urgency and flow state) sharing one row without competing. This pattern repeats everywhere.",
      "f4": " — how this system separates \"document\" from \"app\": different surface, different ink, print styles included.",
      "f5": " — where everything lives at the end of the day. Drag its splitters; reload; notice it remembered.",
      "rulesTitle": "The five rules (the short version)",
      "r1b": "Severity means severity.",
      "r1s1": " clinic-* reds/ambers/greens are clinical judgment. Flow, categories and navigation use ",
      "r1s2": " — never the other way around.",
      "r2b": "Color never works alone.",
      "r2": " Every severity signal carries a glyph, letter or shape. Check any Monochrome story.",
      "r3b": "Patient identity is never bare.",
      "r3": " MRN + DOB render in every density and every locale, always.",
      "r4b": "Names, ages and dates are formatted, never concatenated.",
      "r4": " Thai and Japanese orderings are real and handled.",
      "r5b": "Numbers wear tabular figures.",
      "r5s1": " Anything countable gets ",
      "r5s2": ", so columns compare downward.",
      "n1": "The long version — gotchas, wiring patterns, how to choose a component — lives in ",
      "n2": ". The spec that adjudicated every decision lives in "
    },
    "intro": {
      "title": "Shin Medical UI — Design System",
      "leadPre": "A mobile-first task manager, expressed as a design system on top of ",
      "leadMid": " primitives. This Storybook is the ",
      "specWord": "spec",
      "leadPost": ": every component, state, and token the products are allowed to use lives here.",
      "contractTitle": "The contract",
      "c1Strong": "Tokens",
      "c1s1": " — the palette lives once in ",
      "c1s2": " (",
      "c1s3": " block), documented in ",
      "c1s4": ". Components use generated utilities (",
      "c1s5": "…), never hardcoded hex values.",
      "c2Strong": "Components",
      "c2s1": " — ",
      "c2s2": " are untouched shadcn primitives; ",
      "c2s3": " are the Things-flavored design-system components. Every ",
      "c2s4": " component has a story here showing its states.",
      "c3Strong": "Implementation",
      "c3s1": " — the Rails views (",
      "c3s2": ", …) may only compose what this spec defines.",
      "verifyTitle": "Spec ↔ implementation verification",
      "v1": " (Playwright — ",
      "v2": ") loads the live Rails routes and asserts, per route, that computed styles match the tokens above and that key component invariants hold (19px circular checkboxes, sidebar palette, separator blues, badge red…). It also cross-checks ",
      "v3": " against ",
      "v4": " and smoke-loads the Storybook stories. Run it whenever you touch tokens, ",
